Jijo,

why don't you just do it in R? That should be much faster than sending things around - rough example (untested):

static void setRouteConstraints(RConnection rconn, int [] routes, int value) {
        rconn.assign("routes", routes);
        rconn.eval(
"for (i in routes) {\n" +
"    cpos <- match(i, objList)\n" +
"    if (is.na(cpos)) cpos <- objective.length + i + 1L\n" +
"    this <- constraints[[as.character(i)]]\n" +
"    if (is.null(this)) this <- rep(0L, total)\n" +
"    this[cpos] <- " + value + "L\n" +
"    constraints[[as.character(i)]] <- this\n" +
"}", null, false);
}

public static void equalityConstraint(RConnection rconn){ for (Node node : residualNodes) {
        rconn.eval("constraints <- new.env()", null, false);
        // collect the incoming route IDs (you may have a better way ...)
        int incoming[] = new int[node.incomingRoutes.length];
        int i = 0;
        for (Route route : node.incomingRoutes) incoming[i++] = route.id;
        // set the constraints
        setRouteConstraints(rconn, incoming, 1);
        // same for outgoing
        inf outgoing[] = new int[node.outgoingRoutes.length];
        i = 0;
        for (Route route : node.outgoingRoutes) outgoing[i++] = route.id;
        setRouteConstraints(rconn, outgoing, -1);
        rconn.eval("for (constraint in as.list(constraints)) add.constraint(lprec, constraint, '=', 0)", null, false); } }// Route constraint}

The other approach would be to simply load objList into Java as a hash and construct the constraint vectors on the Java side - and then push them directly. That could be possibly the fastest ...

Cheers,
Simon


